# Source, prior-work, and scope audit

Checked 30 September 2026. No priority claim is made.

## Exact target

W. K. Hayman and E. F. Lingham, *Research Problems in Function Theory*,
[arXiv:1809.07200v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200), 21 September 2018,
Problem 5.51, printed p.105. The complete PDF was retrieved, and this page
was visually checked. The problem attributes the request to F. Holland.
It takes existence as known and asks for an explicit construction. Its update
reports no progress known to the compilers; that dated statement is not a
current comprehensive status certificate.

The candidate preserves the exact disk, Blaschke-product, zero-at-origin,
and Bloch-Cayley-transform requirements. A merely inner function or an
unspecified universal covering map would not complete this construction.

## Established methods and analytic inputs

1. Alicia Cantón, *Singular measures and the little Bloch space*, Publicacions
   Matemàtiques 42 (1998), 211–222.
   [DOI](https://doi.org/10.5565/PUBLMAT_42198_11),
   [full primary PDF](https://mat.uab.cat/pubmat/fitxers/download/FileType:pdf/FolderName:v42(1)/FileName:42198_11.pdf).
   The full paper was read. Its Section 2 uses four-adic martingales, directed
   endpoint choices to control adjacent densities, and barriers. It seeks
   stronger little-Zygmund and symmetry properties with decreasing step sizes.
   The candidate instead specifies constant unit steps, absorption only at
   zero, and all tie-breaking rules. The method family is prior work.
   Cantón explicitly credits the earlier work of Kahane and others.
2. A. B. Aleksandrov, J. M. Anderson, and A. Nicolau, *Inner functions, Bloch
   spaces and symmetric measures*, Proceedings London Mathematical Society
   (3) 79 (1999), 318–352.
   [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1112/S002461159901196X),
   [full author PDF](https://mat.uab.cat/~artur/data/innerfunctions,blochspacesand.pdf).
   Theorem 2 and Section 2 already establish stronger prescribed-decay
   existence results for interpolating Blaschke products by covering maps.
   The proof of Theorem 3.5 on printed p.333, visually checked, states the
   Herglotz–Zygmund criterion used in the candidate and cites Pommerenke's
   monograph. This criterion, rather than the stronger construction, is the
   analytic input for the new explicit recursion.
3. Joan J. Carmona and Juan J. Donaire, *The converse of Fatou's theorem for
   Zygmund measures*, Pacific Journal of Mathematics 191 (1999), 207–222.
   [DOI](https://doi.org/10.2140/pjm.1999.191.207),
   [full published PDF](https://msp.org/pjm/1999/191-2/pjm-v191-n2-p02-p.pdf).
   Printed pp.207–208 define the ordinary derivative using arbitrary shrinking
   intervals containing the point and recall Loomis's positive-measure converse
   Fatou theorem. The finite nontangential-limit hypothesis, positivity, and
   ordinary versus symmetric density distinction are essential. This result
   rules out a singular inner factor in the specified candidate.
4. Artur Nicolau, *Contractive analytic self-mappings of the disc*, 2026
   preprint on the author's publication page.
   [Author page](https://mat.uab.cat/~artur/),
   [full PDF](https://mat.uab.cat/~artur/data/perlaweb.pdf).
   The introduction and stated contractivity/Clark-measure results were checked
   as a current-literature gate. No full proof audit of this paper is claimed,
   and its contractivity theorem is not used to prove the candidate.

Kahane's original reference is *Trois notes sur les ensembles parfaits
linéaires*, L'Enseignement Mathématique 15 (1969), 185–192. Its full-text
download returned an HTML challenge, so no claim to have inspected the complete
original is made. The method attribution above is supported by Cantón's full
paper. The candidate proves its own recursion estimates rather than assuming
an unchecked version of Kahane's construction.

The ordinary inner-factorization and singular-measure boundary facts are also
standard inputs. The proof identifies exactly how they are used; innerness
alone is never taken to imply that the function is a Blaschke product.

## Prior-attempt and novelty gates

The pinned dataset revision is
`37e53eabe540fb458758e198be61634bd02ee008`. The complete numeric record and
uniquely matching imported report were read. The imported report is third-party
source triage, not a prior attempt by this repository's author.

Before the attempt, the live queue had rank 78, queued, 0/5. Exact-ID and
Problem 5.51 PR searches across all states, remote-branch checks, attempt-path
history, local folders, and related-target metadata found no earlier campaign
attempt. No existing history was reset.

Current searches covered the exact question, Bloch Cayley transforms,
four-adic smooth measures, Frostman shifts, and covering-map constructions.
No claim of historical priority for the specified recursion or its application
is supported by this bounded audit. The question here is whether the explicit
construction is correct and satisfies the source request.
